  "methodology": "Other",
  "methodology_other": "Combined Drought Indicator (CDI)\r\n\r\nThe Combined Drought Indicator (CDI) integrates anomalies of precipitation, soil moisture, and vegetation greenness (see CDI Conceptual Framework, Table 2a). It uses three drought indicators and two thematic masks implemented in EDO.\r\n\r\n1. Drought Indicators\r\na. Standardized Precipitation Index (SPI)\r\n\r\nA well-known indicator recommended by the World Meteorological Organization (WMO) for meteorological drought monitoring.\r\n\r\nQuantifies the deviation of accumulated precipitation over a given timescale compared to a reference baseline (McKee et al., 1993; Edwards and McKee, 1997).\r\n\r\nCDI uses two timescales:\r\n\r\nSPI-1 (30 days) – indicates Extreme Meteorological Drought when value < -2\r\n\r\nSPI-3 (90 days) – indicates Moderate Meteorological Drought when value < -1\r\n\r\nCalculated using ERA5 (European ReAnalysis 5th Generation) precipitation data (Hersbach et al., 2017).\r\n\r\nb. Soil Moisture Index Anomaly (SMI)\r\n\r\nDerived from anomalies of estimated daily soil moisture content, expressed as a standardized Soil Moisture Index (SMI).\r\n\r\nSMI is a product of the JRC LISFLOOD hydrological model (de Roo et al., 2000; Laguardia and Niemeyer, 2008).\r\n\r\nThe current SMI product is based on EFAS (European Flood Awareness System) version 5.0, operational since 20 September 2023.\r\n\r\nc. FAPAR Anomaly\r\n\r\nFAPAR (Fraction of Absorbed Photosynthetically Active Radiation) measures the fraction of incoming solar radiation absorbed by vegetation for photosynthesis.\r\n\r\nSatellite-derived FAPAR anomalies effectively detect and assess drought impacts on various land cover types (Gobron et al., 2005).\r\n\r\n2. Thematic Masks\r\na. Crop Masks\r\n\r\nAddress seasonal shifts from vegetation to bare soil after harvest that affect FAPAR anomalies.\r\n\r\nPrevents misinterpretation of bare-soil signals as drought stress.\r\n\r\nComprises 10-day gridded binary maps, where each pixel indicates presence or absence of crops during the growing season.\r\n\r\nEnsures that analysis of negative FAPAR anomalies in cropland areas is restricted to the growing season (see Table 2b).\r\n\r\nb. Snow Masks\r\n\r\nPrevent unreliable soil moisture readings under snow cover.\r\n\r\nThe snow mask functions similarly to the crop mask, excluding affected pixels from CDI computations.\r\n\r\nImplemented through a dynamic snow cover masking process, using 10-day gridded binary maps that identify the presence or absence of snow.\r\n\r\nWhen snow is detected, corresponding soil moisture and FAPAR anomalies are excluded from the CDI (see Table 2c).",
  "name": "afghanistan-combined-drought-indicator-cdi",
  "notes": "The Combined Drought Indicator (CDI) dataset identifies different drought classes and their extent, focusing on vegetated areas (at risk, affected, and recovering). It integrates three complementary indicators — Standardized Precipitation Index (SPI), Soil Moisture Index Anomaly (SMA), and FAPAR anomaly — to capture meteorological, agricultural, and vegetation drought conditions.\r\n\r\nThe dataset consists of raster maps with a spatial resolution of ~5 km at the Equator, using WGS84 (EPSG:4326) as the coordinate system. Data are available from 2012 to the present, updated every 10 days, which is suitable for monitoring short-term crop stress.\r\n\r\nProduced operationally by the European Drought Observatory (EDO), the CDI maps are freely accessible online.\r\nRecent updates include:\r\n\r\nCDIv3 (March 2023): integration of static crop masks to limit analysis to active growing seasons.\r\n\r\nCDIv4 (June 2024): introduction of dynamic snow masks and a new soil moisture input based on EFAS v5.\r\n\r\nMore: https://drought.emergency.copernicus.eu/data/factsheets/factsheet_combinedDroughtIndicator_v4.pdf",
